#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bench_docs_probe.py — scratchpad probe: how well do local vision models read German documents?

In the spirit of docs/nas-document-ai.md § 5 (bench_docs, not bench_vision): a
deterministic, self-graded corpus of synthetic German document pages (invoice
KIE, semicolon-CSV table, prose transcription, absent-field fabrication trap),
each with a text-only twin, rendered with PIL at real document DPI. Graders are
pure functions (CER, field-F1, cell-F1, restraint) with a --self-test that
proves the graders PASS perfect replies and FAIL known-wrong ones before any
model is contacted.

Prompt styles (documented honestly):
  style=chat (default)  measures the MODEL'S OWN extraction: the task prompt is
                        sent with the image and the reply is graded directly
                        (JSON / CSV / text).
  style=glm             for OCR-native models (GLM-OCR): every image case is
                        sent with the single prompt 'Text Recognition:' and the
                        raw recognition output is then put through OUR
                        deterministic extraction (regex field-lifting for
                        kie/absent, table parsing with a whitespace-column
                        fallback, CER for transcribe). So style=glm measures
                        recognition + this harness's extraction, NOT the
                        model's extraction. The same deterministic extraction
                        is also the fallback for any chat reply whose JSON does
                        not parse.

This is a scratchpad probe, not the repo's future bench_docs.py.
"""

